# OPUS GUBERNATIO PARTICIPANT PACKAGE og-participant-2026.08-v3
#
# SUCCESSOR to og-participant-2026.08-v2, whose checksum record is preserved unchanged at
# code_audit/run28_closure_participant_package_checksums.sha256, and to og-participant-2026.08-v1
# at code_audit/run12_participant_package_checksums.sha256. Neither predecessor is rewritten.
#
# WHY v3 EXISTS. The Run-28 closure's SECOND pass applied the owner's A1.1 naming decision --
# `Monte Carlo EAC` becomes `Monte Carlo EAC Forecast` -- to the current naming authority and
# re-propagated it. Eleven files inside this package carry that name and their bytes moved after
# the v2 record was taken. This is the successor that records them.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS ALSO CORRECTS, STATED PLAINLY. The second pass did NOT create a successor at
# the time: it REGENERATED THE v2 RECORD IN PLACE over those eleven files. A package record
# rewritten to agree with the tree describes the tree and not the package it names, which is
# exactly the staleness this programme found in the Run-12 v1 record and exactly the thing a
# freeze exists to prevent. The v2 record has been restored to the bytes it carried when v2 was
# frozen, at commit 0293dc5, and this file is the successor that should have been created then.
#
# WHAT CHANGED FROM v2: display strings only, all of them the same module name. Proved by
# normalisation rather than asserted -- server/tools/test_run28_participant_packages.py maps each
# current file back through the rename and requires BYTE IDENTITY with the v2-era blob. The
# participant decision sequence -- evidence, preliminary assessment, confidence, preliminary
# lock, reveal, final action, final lock, next period -- randomization, reveal timing, lock
# enforcement, server authority, append-only records and treatment logic are all unchanged, and
# every file that implements any of them is byte-identical to its v2 bytes.
